{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And they hid them.</b> The law of Torah regarding the destruction of idolatry (ע\"ג) is not by burial, but rather one grinds it up and scatters it to the wind or casts it into the sea, just as Moses did in the matter of the calf, as it is written (Exodus 32): ״ויטחן עד אשר דק ויזר על פני המים״ — and he ground it until it was fine and strewed it upon the face of the waters. But this [burial] indicates that it [the idolatry] was permitted, and since it had been annulled it became permitted, as we have said; therefore burial suffices. Or one may interpret that even if it was actual idolatry (ע\"ג), which is forbidden for benefit, he did not have the ability to take it to the Salt Sea, for he was on the road and this was impossible for him; and he also could not burn it, lest he be delayed by this and the peoples around him gather against him; therefore he needed to hide them, for this was the easiest thing in his hand."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:43.204450118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}